# No explainer copy in the software

Set by Nina, 2 Sep 2026, during the Farmer J day plan build.

The screen states facts about the user's data. It never explains how the
product works, why a number is what it is, or what will happen when a button
is pressed. That explanation belongs in the chat reply and the handover
notes for Nina and Ed, not in the UI.

## Never add

- Footnotes or paragraphs under tables ("Each cell is editable. Use the
  steppers to...").
- Intro copy at the top of panels and drawers ("Orders arrive from the
  catering platform and land on...").
- Section subtitles that describe the section ("Struck-through days are
  left out of the average.").
- Consequence sentences on buttons and banners ("Approving the window
  covers Wed to Sun; each morning you can still change the day.").
- Feedback that narrates the mechanism ("Every row it touched has
  re-derived."). State the event only: "Hines cancelled."
- Provenance or demo tags on data rows ("Demo recipe", "Catering"). If the
  build needs to remember which data is invented, keep it in a code comment
  or a note the user does not see on the working screen.

## Allowed

- Numbers, names, dates, units, statuses.
- A unit label in a column header ("portions", "cast irons", "gastronorms").
- A sub-line that is another fact about the same cell ("≈ 22 round cast
  irons", "20 buffet boxes", "fc 12").
- A pill only when it changes what the user does next ("Edited", "Locked",
  "Cancelled").
- Working shown as ledger rows (label, value), never as prose.

## When tempted to explain

Turn the sentence into a fact row, or delete it and write the explanation
in the chat reply to Nina instead. If a screen cannot be understood without
the sentence, the layout is wrong; fix the layout.

## Related conventions from the same build

- One unit vocabulary per screen. On production screens: demand in
  portions, lines in containers, catering in containers with ordered boxes
  underneath, carry-over in containers, output in batches, money in pounds.
  Grams and kilos only where something is weighed on a scale (prep list,
  cascade), and nowhere else.
- Containers are named by size only: small, medium, large, extra-large,
  as many as the methods have fill sizes. Never "cast iron", "gastronorm",
  "GN" or any other shape or material word on screen. The kitchen's own
  names are a Setup setting. Prep and kitchen vessels use plain words the
  same way: "prep tray", "10 litre tub", "oven tray", "blue box".
- Prep quantities are in the unit written on the label: batches for kits,
  kilos for dressings and mixes (the 1 to 18 kg matrix), packs where the
  rule is "open the can, prep the can", bags for parsley. Edify's number
  stays visible as "fc" under any figure the manager types over, with a
  clear action next to it. Same pattern as the Day plan steppers.
- New Farmer J screens reuse an existing Pret chassis. Day plan sits on
  the Plan grid; Prep list sits on the Run sheet (sticky toolbar with a
  segmented switcher and Print, cards tiled in a grid, name left and
  quantity right). Do not invent a third layout language.
- A column that grows with the data (one column per catering order) is an
  Excel workaround. Consolidate into one column and put the detail in a
  drawer.
